      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-221562",
      ":question/problem": "Prove by contradiction that $14\u2224(6n + 5)$ for any $n \u2208 Z$.\n\nYou may assume without proof that $6nmod14 \u2208 {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}$ for all $n$.\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that $14$ {{field-1}} $(6n + 5)$. Then, {{field-2}}, where $k$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$6n + 5 = 14k$ {{field-3}} {{field-4}}\n\nHence, we have that $6nmod14 =$ {{field-5}}.\n\nWe're given that $6nmod14 \u2208 R = {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}$ for all $n$. Therefore, we have a contradiction since\n{{field-6}}\n{{field-7}}."
